# Single-server quickstart — copy to `.env.single` next to the compose file.
# NEVER commit the real file (gitignored).
#
#   docker compose --env-file .env.single -f docker-compose.single.yml up -d
#
# This template is for deploy/docker-compose.single.yml ONLY — one machine,
# reached by IP, nginx on :80, no reverse proxy. Running behind Traefik with a
# real domain? Use deploy/.env.prod.example instead; it asks for DOMAIN /
# S3_DOMAIN and this one does not.
#
# The two stacks used to share one template, which put the operator of each in
# front of the other's variables: SERVER_IP shipped COMMENTED OUT even though
# this stack refuses to start without it, while DOMAIN shipped ACTIVE even
# though this stack never reads it. Splitting them is what fixed that.

# ── The two you MUST fill ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The address browsers will use. Presigned S3 URLs embed it, so it has to be
# reachable FROM THE BROWSER: a public IP or a hostname. Not `localhost`, not a
# docker-internal name. Compose refuses to resolve without it (`${SERVER_IP:?}`).
SERVER_IP=

# Pick a real release: https://github.com/weironz/mica/releases
# DELIBERATELY EMPTY, and the compose file asks for it as `${MICA_VERSION:?...}`
# — so an empty value makes `docker compose` refuse to start and say so. That is
# the point: a quickstart that silently installs whichever tag happened to be
# newest when this file was written is worse than one that asks.
# Do NOT use `:latest` — the release workflow only pushes `:v<version>`.
MICA_VERSION=

# Where to pull from. Defaults to Docker Hub.
# MICA_REGISTRY=willdockerhub

# ── Secrets — every one optional, but NOT equally safe ───────────────────────
# You can start this stack without setting any of these. What that costs differs
# per credential, so read the S3 one before deciding to leave it alone:
#
#   JWT_SECRET         self-minted by the server. No default exists to leak.
#   POSTGRES_PASSWORD  defaulted, and safe: this compose file publishes no
#                      postgres port, so nothing outside the stack can present it.
#   S3_ACCESS_KEY /    defaulted, and NOT safe on a public node. rustfs
#   S3_SECRET_KEY      publishes :9000 to the internet on purpose (browsers
#                      presign against it directly), so the default pair — which
#                      is written in this repository — lets anyone who reads it
#                      read and write your files.

# OPTIONAL since 0.13.16. Leave it blank: the server mints a random key on first
# boot and keeps it in the database (the `jwt_secret` row of `server_secrets`),
# so sessions survive restarts and upgrades and every instance's key is its own.
#
# Set it only to supply your own. A value you DO set is still held to the strict
# bar — template placeholders, or anything under 32 characters, refuse to start.
JWT_SECRET=

# OPTIONAL. Defaults to `mica`. Set it only if you have exposed the database
# beyond the compose network. If you do set it, set it BEFORE the first start:
# the volume keeps whatever password initdb ran with, so changing it later locks
# the api out until you ALTER USER by hand.
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# OPTIONAL, defaulting to `mica` / `mica-default-not-a-secret`, which are
# PUBLISHED IN THIS REPOSITORY. Leaving them alone means anyone who knows that
# constant can read and write this instance's files over :9000. The api warns
# about it in the log on every production start (`default_s3_secret_in_use`).
#
# Set both, BEFORE the first start, on any instance reachable by people you do
# not trust:
#   openssl rand -hex 12    # S3_ACCESS_KEY
#   openssl rand -hex 32    # S3_SECRET_KEY
# Changing them later also means updating what rustfs already stored — easiest
# on a fresh volume, so decide now rather than after uploading files.
# S3_ACCESS_KEY=
# S3_SECRET_KEY=

# ── Accounts: public self-registration ───────────────────────────────────────
# CLOSED unless you open it here: only an explicit true/1/yes/on opens
# POST /auth/register, and anything else — unset, empty, or a typo — keeps it
# 403ing. A brand-new instance can still create its very FIRST account, so a
# fresh install is usable without ever touching this line.
# MICA_REGISTRATION_ENABLED=true

# ── App CORS (browser cross-origin reads) ────────────────────────────────────
# Empty/unset = deny all cross-origin (the web app is served same-origin with
# /api, so it needs no grant). Set ONLY if a separate origin must call the API.
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://other.example.com

# ── Password-reset email (see docs/password-reset.md) ────────────────────────
# Default backend "log" writes the reset link to the SERVER LOG and sends
# nothing — the flow still works (read the link from the logs), so leaving this
# unset is a valid "no email yet" state.
# MICA_APP_BASE_URL=http://203.0.113.10        # origin the emailed link points at
# MICA_MAIL_BACKEND=directmail
# MICA_MAIL_FROM=noreply@mail.example.com      # verified DirectMail sender address
# MICA_MAIL_FROM_NAME=Mica
# MICA_MAIL_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# MICA_MAIL_ACCESS_KEY_SECRET=

# ── DOMAIN / S3_DOMAIN: deliberately absent ──────────────────────────────────
# They belong to the Traefik stack (deploy/docker-compose.yml + .env.prod.example)
# and docker-compose.single.yml never reads them. Setting them here does nothing.
